.IX Title "Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer 3pm"
.TH Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer 3pm 2026-09-16 "perl v5.40.1" "User Contributed Perl Documentation"
.\" For nroff, turn off justification.  Always turn off hyphenation; it makes
.\" way too many mistakes in technical documents.
.if n .ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer \- Tokenize/modify/filter text.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\&    my $strings = $analyzer\->split($text);
\&
\&    package SimpleAnalyzer;
\&    use base qw( Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer );
\&    sub new {
\&        return shift\->SUPER::new;
\&    }
\&    sub equals {
\&        my ( $self, $other ) = @_;
\&        return $other\->isa(_\|_PACKAGE_\|_);
\&    }
\&    sub transform {
\&        my ( $self, $inversion ) = @_;
\&        while ( my $token = $inversion\->next ) {
\&            my $text = $token\->get_text;
\&            # Transform text...
\&            $token\->set_text($text);
\&        }
\&        $inversion\->reset;
\&        return $inversion;
\&    }
\&
\&    package AnalyzerWithMemberVars;
\&    use base qw( Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer );
\&    our %foo;
\&    sub new {
\&        my $self = shift\->SUPER::new;
\&        return $self\->init( { @_ } );
\&    }
\&    sub init {
\&        my ( $self, $args ) = @_;
\&        $foo{$$self} = $args\->{foo};
\&        return $self;
\&    }
\&    sub DESTROY {
\&        my $self = shift;
\&        delete $foo{$$self};
\&        $self\->SUPER::DESTROY;
\&    }
\&    sub equals {
\&        my ( $self, $other ) = @_;
\&        return $other\->isa(_\|_PACKAGE_\|_)
\&               && $foo{$$self} eq $foo{$$other};
\&    }
\&    sub dump {
\&        my $self = shift;
\&        my $dump = $self\->SUPER::dump;
\&        $dump\->{foo} = $foo{$$self};
\&        return $dump;
\&    }
\&    sub load {
\&        my ( $self, $dump ) = @_;
\&        my $loaded = $self\->SUPER::load($dump);
\&        return $loaded\->init($dump);
\&    }
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
An Analyzer is a filter which processes text, transforming it from one form
into another.  For instance, an analyzer might break up a long text into
smaller pieces (RegexTokenizer), or it
might perform case folding to facilitate case-insensitive search
(Normalizer).
.PP
Subclasses of Analyzer must override the \fBequals()\fR method.
.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.IX Header "CONSTRUCTORS"
.SS new
.IX Subsection "new"
.Vb 9
\&    package MyAnalyzer;
\&    use base qw( Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer );
\&    our %foo;
\&    sub new {
\&        my $self = shift\->SUPER::new;
\&        my %args = @_;
\&        $foo{$$self} = $args{foo};
\&        return $self;
\&    }
.Ve
.PP
Abstract constructor.  Takes no arguments.
.SH "ABSTRACT METHODS"
.IX Header "ABSTRACT METHODS"
.SS transform
.IX Subsection "transform"
.Vb 1
\&    my $inversion = $analyzer\->transform($inversion);
.Ve
.PP
Take a single Inversion as input
and returns an Inversion, either the same one (presumably transformed
in some way), or a new one.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBinversion\fR \- An inversion.
.SH METHODS
.IX Header "METHODS"
.SS transform_text
.IX Subsection "transform_text"
.Vb 1
\&    my $inversion = $analyzer\->transform_text($text);
.Ve
.PP
Kick off an analysis chain, creating an Inversion from string input.
The default implementation simply creates an initial Inversion with a
single Token, then calls \fBtransform()\fR, but occasionally subclasses will
provide an optimized implementation which minimizes string copies.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBtext\fR \- A string.
.SS split
.IX Subsection "split"
.Vb 1
\&    my $arrayref = $analyzer\->split($text);
.Ve
.PP
Analyze text and return an array of token texts.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBtext\fR \- A string.
.SS dump
.IX Subsection "dump"
.Vb 1
\&    my $obj = $analyzer\->dump();
.Ve
.PP
Dump the analyzer as hash.
.PP
Subclasses should call \fBdump()\fR on the superclass. The returned
object is a hash which should be populated with parameters of
the analyzer.
.PP
Returns: A hash containing a description of the analyzer.
.SS load
.IX Subsection "load"
.Vb 1
\&    my $obj = $analyzer\->load($dump);
.Ve
.PP
Reconstruct an analyzer from a dump.
.PP
Subclasses should first call \fBload()\fR on the superclass. The
returned object is an analyzer which should be reconstructed by
setting the dumped parameters from the hash contained in \f(CW\*(C`dump\*(C'\fR.
.PP
Note that the invocant analyzer is unused.
.IP \(bu 4
\&\fBdump\fR \- A hash.
.PP
Returns: An analyzer.
.SH INHERITANCE
.IX Header "INHERITANCE"
Lucy::Analysis::Analyzer isa Clownfish::Obj.
